The study addresses challenges associated with immigrant selection and skill transferability using detailed wage data from the New Immigrant Survey (NIS) and additional sources, such as the Mexican Migration Project and the Latin American Migration Project. The paper explains that immigrants bring their human capital but not their physical capital or TFP when moving to the U.S., thus their wage gains at migration can separate human capital's impact from other factors.

Key findings include that immigrants from poorer countries (GDP per worker less than one-quarter of the U.S. level) experience an average wage gain at migration equal to 38 percent of the total GDP per worker gap, attributing the remaining income difference primarily to human capital. The wage gain is more pronounced for less educated immigrants, indicating imperfect substitution between skilled and unskilled labor, which further refines the human capital share estimates.

